  "summary": "The San Francisco Standard reports that SFPD allowed out-of-state law enforcement agencies to run more than 1.6 million searches of its Flock license-plate reader data between August 2024 and February 2025, in apparent violation of California sanctuary and ALPR-sharing laws. At least 19 searches were tied to ICE-related purposes, including by the Franklin County (GA) Sheriff, Dallas PD, Milford (MA) PD, and CHP. SFPD says outside-California access has since been cut off and pledged a transparency portal.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "San Francisco police let out-of-state cops run more than 1.6 million illegal searches of the city's license-plate reader database \u2014 including at least 19 that were marked as related to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "According to a recent audit of our network, it appears agencies outside California had the ability to query SFPD's Flock system for a limited period of time",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "The logs show the SFPD's data was searched at least 19 times for \"ICE Fugitive,\" \"Assist ICE,\" or specific ICE detention numbers.",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    },
    {
      "quote": "The Police Commission and Mayor Lurie need to codify penalties for anyone sharing San Francisco's ALPR data with other agencies for immigration enforcement purposes",
      "paragraph_idx": 26
    }
  ],
